## Scope

This page treats a finite population's unit values as fixed and places the
probability law on sampled subsets. It defines population totals and means,
design expectation, first- and second-order inclusion probabilities, and the
observed-record experiment. A superpopulation model that randomizes the unit
values is a separate probability mechanism.

The Horvitz–Thompson total and mean are design-unbiased when every unit has
positive first-order inclusion probability. If a unit has zero probability of
selection, changing only its value leaves the observed-data law unchanged
while changing the total; consequently the total is not identifiable and no
observed-sample estimator is unbiased for every finite population.

The page makes no variance-estimation, normal-inference, or model-adequacy
claim. Fixed-design regression holds its design matrix fixed and does not by
itself specify a probability law for selecting finite-population units.
